From d01cc4fe8b911efffa221f139a41a2c880e73308 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tyler South <tsouth2@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 10:18:36 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Keep each display window's pointer on its own output

With more than one display, the launcher now gives every display window its
own virtio tablet, bound to that window's head, with the serial
try-omarchy-head-N for the window that shows Virtual-(N+1). QEMU reports a
window's pointer as 0 to 100% of that window, and Hyprland spreads an
absolute pointer over the box around all outputs. With one tablet, moving
across window 1 crossed the whole layout, so the pointer was only on screen
over half of each window (#424).

Hyprland binds tablets and touch screens to an output, but not a pointer
device like this one, so each tablet gets a libinput calibration matrix
that maps it onto its own output within that box. head-tablets computes the
matrices from the monitors of the active seat0 session's Hyprland (not a
nested one or another account's), by output name, with the output's position,
mode, scale and rotation, and a mirror's tablet goes to its source. It
writes them to /run/try-omarchy/head-tablets, where the new udev rule
imports them, and announces a tablet again when its matrix changes, which is
how libinput reads a new one. It looks again on Hyprland's monitor and
config reload events, a few times after a GPU hotplug (a window resize
changes the mode), and every five seconds. Edges land on the centre of the
output's edge pixels, so the pointer never slips onto the next output.

udev starts the helper for the first per-display tablet, so a guest with one
display, or a launcher that gives one tablet, never runs it. Compatibility
revision 75 delivers it to existing disks.
